```java
public interface RetryPolicy {
    int maxAttempts();
    boolean isRetryable(Exception e);
    Duration backoffFor(int attempt);
}

public <T> T retry(Supplier<T> operation, RetryPolicy policy) {
    Exception last = null;
    for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= policy.maxAttempts(); attempt++) {
        try { return operation.get(); }
        catch (Exception e) {
            if (!policy.isRetryable(e)) throw e;
            last = e;
            sleep(policy.backoffFor(attempt));
        }
    }
    throw new RetryExhaustedException(last);
}
```

Notice `isRetryable` is checked before sleeping — a non-retryable exception fails fast on the first attempt, matching [retries, timeouts, and backoff](retries-timeouts-and-backoff).

```java
public Duration backoffFor(int attempt) {
    long base = (long) (baseDelayMs * Math.pow(2, attempt - 1));
    long jitter = ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(0, base / 2 + 1);
    return Duration.ofMillis(base + jitter);
}
```

A fixed exponential backoff still causes a **thundering herd of retries**: if a thousand clients all failed at the same instant (a brief outage), they all retry at exactly the same computed delay, hammering the recovering service in synchronized waves. Adding random jitter spreads those retries out in time, smoothing the load instead of re-creating the spike.
